1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,977
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is Intro to Computer

2
00:00:04,977 --> 00:00:09,815
Science, Unit 5: Networks and the
Internet. Let's find out what really

3
00:00:09,815 --> 00:00:12,096
happens when you open a web page.

4
00:00:13,269 --> 00:00:18,110
Every time you send a message or load a
video, your device talks to other

5
00:00:18,110 --> 00:00:18,773
computers.

6
00:00:19,223 --> 00:00:22,380
To do that, they need to be connected.
That's a network.

7
00:00:23,530 --> 00:00:27,541
A network is two or more devices linked so
they can share data.

8
00:00:27,991 --> 00:00:33,379
The devices are called nodes. Phones,
laptops, printers and servers can all be

9
00:00:33,379 --> 00:00:33,794
nodes.

10
00:00:34,944 --> 00:00:40,637
The links can be wired, like ethernet
cables and fiber optic lines, or wireless,

11
00:00:40,637 --> 00:00:43,413
like Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and cell signals.

12
00:00:43,863 --> 00:00:48,646
Wired links are usually faster and
steadier. Wireless links let you move

13
00:00:48,646 --> 00:00:49,111
around.

14
00:00:50,261 --> 00:00:56,137
A small network, like the one in your home
or school, is called a LAN, a local area

15
00:00:56,137 --> 00:00:56,704
network.

16
00:00:57,154 --> 00:01:03,042
Networks that cover cities or countries
are called WANs, wide area networks.

17
00:01:04,192 --> 00:01:09,273
Inside a home, a router connects your
devices to each other and to your internet

18
00:01:09,273 --> 00:01:09,845
provider.

19
00:01:10,295 --> 00:01:15,741
The internet is a network of networks:
millions of LANs joined together around

20
00:01:15,741 --> 00:01:16,439
the world.

21
00:01:17,589 --> 00:01:22,581
Networks are measured by bandwidth, how
much data they can send per second, in

22
00:01:22,581 --> 00:01:23,605
bits per second.

23
00:01:24,055 --> 00:01:29,111
They're also measured by latency, how long
a single message takes to arrive.

24
00:01:30,261 --> 00:01:36,752
Your turn. A school's network connects
every computer in one building. Is it a

25
00:01:36,752 --> 00:01:37,834
LAN or a WAN?

26
00:01:38,284 --> 00:01:39,564
Pause the video now.

27
00:01:40,014 --> 00:01:44,132
It covers one building, so it's a LAN, a
local area network.

28
00:01:44,582 --> 00:01:48,208
Next, we'll see how data actually travels:
in packets.

29
00:01:49,359 --> 00:01:53,775
When you send a photo across the internet,
it doesn't travel in one piece.

30
00:01:54,225 --> 00:01:56,785
It's chopped into small chunks called
packets.

31
00:01:57,935 --> 00:02:01,647
Each packet holds a small piece of the
data, plus a header.

32
00:02:02,097 --> 00:02:06,953
The header is like the outside of an
envelope. It has the sender's address, the

33
00:02:06,953 --> 00:02:10,886
destination address, and a number that
says which piece this is.

34
00:02:12,036 --> 00:02:14,831
Packets travel through devices called
routers.

35
00:02:15,281 --> 00:02:19,983
Each router looks at a packet's
destination, and sends it one step closer,

36
00:02:19,983 --> 00:02:21,190
to the next router.

37
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:25,306
Packets from the same file can take
different paths.

38
00:02:25,756 --> 00:02:31,281
If one route is busy or broken, routers
send packets another way. This makes the

39
00:02:31,281 --> 00:02:35,633
internet fault tolerant: it keeps working
even when parts fail.

40
00:02:36,783 --> 00:02:40,410
Because packets take different paths, they
can arrive out of order.

41
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:46,376
The receiving computer uses the numbers in
the headers to put them back together. If

42
00:02:46,376 --> 00:02:49,265
a packet goes missing, it asks for it
again.

43
00:02:50,415 --> 00:02:55,851
Why not send one big piece? Small packets
share the network fairly, and if one gets

44
00:02:55,851 --> 00:02:58,799
lost, only that small piece has to be
resent.

45
00:02:59,249 --> 00:03:01,404
This idea is called packet switching.

46
00:03:02,554 --> 00:03:07,919
Your turn. A file is split into packets
one, two, three and four. They arrive in

47
00:03:07,919 --> 00:03:11,876
the order three, one, four, two. What does
the receiver do?

48
00:03:12,326 --> 00:03:13,606
Pause the video now.

49
00:03:14,056 --> 00:03:18,984
It uses the packet numbers to put them
back in order: one, two, three, four.

50
00:03:19,434 --> 00:03:24,405
Next, we'll see how packets know where to
go: IP addresses and DNS.

51
00:03:25,555 --> 00:03:30,739
Every packet needs a destination address.
On the internet, that's an IP address.

52
00:03:31,189 --> 00:03:33,066
And DNS is how we find it.

53
00:03:34,216 --> 00:03:38,675
An IP address is a number that identifies
a device on a network.

54
00:03:39,125 --> 00:03:45,426
The common version, IPv4, is four numbers
from zero to two fifty five, separated by

55
00:03:45,426 --> 00:03:50,133
dots, like one ninety two dot one sixty
eight dot one dot ten.

56
00:03:51,283 --> 00:03:56,595
IPv4 has thirty two bits, so there are
about four point three billion addresses.

57
00:03:57,045 --> 00:04:02,101
That's not enough for every device on
Earth. So the newer IPv6 uses one hundred

58
00:04:02,101 --> 00:04:05,813
twenty eight bits, which is far more than
we'll ever need.

59
00:04:06,963 --> 00:04:11,784
People don't remember numbers well. We
type names, like example dot com.

60
00:04:12,234 --> 00:04:17,802
DNS, the domain name system, is the
internet's phone book. It turns a name

61
00:04:17,802 --> 00:04:19,232
into an IP address.

62
00:04:20,382 --> 00:04:25,822
When you type a web address, your computer
asks a DNS server for the IP address.

63
00:04:26,272 --> 00:04:31,586
The server answers, and your computer
sends its packets to that address. Answers

64
00:04:31,586 --> 00:04:35,040
are saved for a while, so the next lookup
is faster.

65
00:04:36,190 --> 00:04:41,011
Your computer also has an IP address, so
replies know where to come back.

66
00:04:41,461 --> 00:04:45,813
At home, your router hands out addresses
to each device that joins.

67
00:04:46,963 --> 00:04:52,446
Your turn. Is two sixty dot one dot one
dot one a valid IPv4 address?

68
00:04:52,896 --> 00:04:54,176
Pause the video now.

69
00:04:54,626 --> 00:05:00,108
No. Each part must be from zero to two
fifty five, and two sixty is too big.

70
00:05:00,558 --> 00:05:03,609
Next, we'll see what happens when you open
a web page.

71
00:05:04,759 --> 00:05:07,362
The internet and the web are not the same
thing.

72
00:05:07,812 --> 00:05:13,353
The internet is the network. The web is
one service that runs on it: pages and

73
00:05:13,353 --> 00:05:15,342
links you open in a browser.

74
00:05:16,492 --> 00:05:19,735
A web address is called a URL. It has
parts.

75
00:05:20,185 --> 00:05:27,422
https is the protocol. example dot com is
the domain. And slash games is the path to

76
00:05:27,422 --> 00:05:29,145
a page on that site.

77
00:05:30,295 --> 00:05:32,727
The web uses a client and server model.

78
00:05:33,177 --> 00:05:38,229
Your browser is the client. It sends a
request. A web server sends back a

79
00:05:38,229 --> 00:05:38,852
response.

80
00:05:40,002 --> 00:05:45,783
The language of that conversation is HTTP,
the hypertext transfer protocol.

81
00:05:46,233 --> 00:05:52,419
A request like GET slash games asks for a
page. The response starts with a status

82
00:05:52,419 --> 00:05:56,772
code. Two hundred means OK. Four oh four
means not found.

83
00:05:57,922 --> 00:06:02,146
The response holds HTML, the code that
describes the page.

84
00:06:02,596 --> 00:06:08,257
The browser reads the HTML, fetches the
images and styles it mentions, and draws

85
00:06:08,257 --> 00:06:09,956
the page on your screen.

86
00:06:11,106 --> 00:06:14,370
HTTPS is HTTP with encryption.

87
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:20,196
The data is scrambled between your browser
and the server, so others on the network

88
00:06:20,196 --> 00:06:23,694
can't read it. Look for it whenever you
log in or pay.

89
00:06:24,844 --> 00:06:29,921
Your turn. You request a page, and the
server answers with status code four oh

90
00:06:29,921 --> 00:06:31,223
four. What happened?

91
00:06:31,673 --> 00:06:32,953
Pause the video now.

92
00:06:33,403 --> 00:06:38,822
Four oh four means not found. The server
is working, but that page doesn't exist.

93
00:06:39,272 --> 00:06:40,957
Next, we'll explain the cloud.

94
00:06:42,107 --> 00:06:45,776
Your photos are in the cloud. Your game
saves are in the cloud.

95
00:06:46,226 --> 00:06:47,784
But where is the cloud, really?

96
00:06:48,934 --> 00:06:51,643
The cloud is just other people's
computers.

97
00:06:52,093 --> 00:06:56,861
They're servers in huge buildings called
data centers, owned by companies, and

98
00:06:56,861 --> 00:06:58,450
reached over the internet.

99
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:04,973
A data center holds thousands of servers
in racks, with powerful cooling, backup

100
00:07:04,973 --> 00:07:07,323
power, and very fast network links.

101
00:07:07,773 --> 00:07:11,037
Big companies run data centers all over
the world.

102
00:07:12,187 --> 00:07:14,384
Cloud services come in a few kinds.

103
00:07:14,834 --> 00:07:20,081
Cloud storage keeps your files, like a
photo backup. Cloud apps run in your

104
00:07:20,081 --> 00:07:25,537
browser, like online documents. And
developers can rent computing power to run

105
00:07:25,537 --> 00:07:26,866
their own programs.

106
00:07:28,016 --> 00:07:33,599
The cloud has big benefits. You can reach
your files from any device. Data is copied

107
00:07:33,599 --> 00:07:38,849
to several places, so one broken drive
doesn't lose it. And you can use as much

108
00:07:38,849 --> 00:07:40,710
computing power as you need.

109
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:45,654
But there are tradeoffs. You need an
internet connection, and you're trusting a

110
00:07:45,654 --> 00:07:46,962
company with your data.

111
00:07:48,112 --> 00:07:52,965
So when an app syncs your work between
your phone and your laptop, it's sending

112
00:07:52,965 --> 00:07:57,264
data to a server in a data center, and
back down to your other device.

113
00:07:58,414 --> 00:08:03,182
Your turn. Your laptop breaks, but your
photos are safe on your new phone. Where

114
00:08:03,182 --> 00:08:04,196
were they stored?

115
00:08:04,646 --> 00:08:05,926
Pause the video now.

116
00:08:06,376 --> 00:08:10,536
In the cloud: on servers in a data center,
reached over the internet.

117
00:08:10,986 --> 00:08:15,551
That's the end of Unit five. Next, we'll
learn how to stay secure online.

118
00:08:16,701 --> 00:08:21,378
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

119
00:08:21,378 --> 00:08:22,653
miss the next lesson.

120
00:08:23,103 --> 00:08:23,999
See you there.

